.TH COMENQ 1 "May 2024" "comenq" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
comenq \- manage queued GitHub pull request comments
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B comenq put
[
.B --now
]
.I owner/repo
.I pr-number
.I comment
.br
.B comenq list
.br
.B comenq bump
.I id
.br
.B comenq bust
.I id
.br
.B comenq del
.I id
.br
.B comenq await
.I id
.br
.B comenq hist
[
.B -n
.I limit
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B comenq
command manages the comment queue of the
.BR comenqd (8)
daemon.
.TP
.B put
Enqueue a comment for the given repository (in
.I owner/repo
format) and pull request.  Prints the comment's deterministic
eight-character identifier and an approximate ETA.  The ETA includes the
random flutter, which is fixed when the comment is enqueued.  By default
the comment waits one full cooldown (plus its flutter) from enqueue even
when the queue is idle; pass
.B --now
to post as soon as the queue allows.
.TP
.B list
Print the schedule of pending comments: identifier, ETA, target pull
request, and the comment text collapsed to a single line of at most 60
characters.
.TP
.B bump
Move the identified comment to the head of the queue.
.TP
.B bust
Move the identified comment to the tail of the queue.
.TP
.B del
Remove the identified comment from the queue.
.TP
.B await
Wait for the identified queued comment to be posted. The identifier must be
the eight-character hexadecimal ID printed by
.B put
or
.BR list .
On success, print the full GitHub comment URL, including its comment fragment,
as the sole line on standard output. Newline-terminated ETA countdowns are
written to standard error, so scripts can capture the URL with
.BR "URL=$(comenq await 1a2b3c4d)" .
An unknown identifier fails before a countdown is printed. Failed posting
attempts leave the comment queued for the daemon to retry after a full cooldown;
.B await
continues waiting through those retries. Deletion, daemon shutdown, connection
loss, or a successful historical post with no recorded URL ends the wait with
an error. Interrupting the client leaves the queue unchanged, and a later
.B await
can still report the URL.
.IP
Countdowns use the ETA format shown by
.B put
and
.BR list ,
followed by
.BR remaining ;
zero is printed as
.BR "due now" .
The report interval is 10 seconds through 60 seconds remaining, 30 seconds
through 10 minutes, 2 minutes through 1 hour, 10 minutes through 6 hours, and
30 minutes for longer waits. Each interval is capped at the remaining wait,
with a one-second minimum.
.TP
.B hist
Print the posting-history log, oldest first: identifier, age, status (
.I ok
or
.IR FAIL ),
the first eight characters of the posting token's hash, target
.IR owner/repo#pr ,
and the comment text collapsed to a single line of at most 60 characters.
Failed attempts also show a one-line description of the error. If nothing
has been recorded, prints a message saying so.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-limit " " \fIlimit\fP
Show only the most recent
.I limit
records, still printed oldest first. By default the whole log is printed.
.RE
.PP
Use
.B --socket
(or the
.B COMENQ_SOCKET
environment variable) to override the Unix Domain Socket path used to
contact the daemon.  When
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is set to a non-empty absolute path, the client first tries
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/comenq/comenq.sock
(a user-hosted daemon) and then
.I /run/comenq/comenq.sock
(the packaged system service), connecting to the first socket that
accepts.  Otherwise, it falls back to the system socket.  A stale socket file
left behind by a daemon that exited without unlinking it is skipped rather
than shadowing a healthy daemon.
.SH EXAMPLES
Send a comment to pull request 42 of example/repo, then inspect the queue:
.PP
.RS
.nf
comenq put example/repo 42 "Queued for review"
comenq list
comenq await 1a2b3c4d
URL=$(comenq await 1a2b3c4d)
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested operation succeeded. For
.BR await ,
the comment URL was received and written.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed, including an unknown or removed await identifier,
posting history without a URL, daemon error or connection failure.
.TP
.B 2
The command line is invalid, including an await identifier that is not exactly
eight hexadecimal characters.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR comenqd (8)
